{"kind":"artifact","text":"The architecture reference states the admitted-time authority rule and the boundary on what the browser may derive, carried into the plain-language rewrite now in main. Exact clean pushed head f6489c78a4e9c11460ebe95e3312f1acd1d0de2c on request/decision-records-ui-recut; local and origin identical by commit SHA and tree hash 340806ed, checkout clean. One file, +92/-1.\n\nTHIS IS THE PART OF THE RECUT THAT WAS NOT MECHANICAL, so I am stating exactly what I did. My own rewrite of this page merged first, as main d8edc000a, and this lane's page was written against the version before it. Both describe the same layers. I checked whether the rewrite had already absorbed this lane's material and it had not: \"Layer 5 and layer 7\", \"what the browser may derive\", \"keep-decision-records.md\" and \"satisfier\" were all absent from main's page. So resolving to either side would have lost something real. I carried the lane's material into the rewritten page by hand instead. The result is 1184 lines: main's 1093-line rewrite intact plus 92 added lines, with a single deletion, the ui/ table row this head deliberately extends. Front matter is byte-identical to main and no citation was added, removed or re-anchored.\n\nWHAT THE ADDED MATERIAL SAYS. The admitted-time authority rule: a ratification is decided by the satisfier on the target kind's definition as it stood when the target statement was admitted, never by whichever definition governs now, which is the rule lifecycle already follows and for the same reason. And the layer 5 to layer 7 boundary: layer 7 may read any field layer 5 projects and combine those fields for presentation, but may not name a relation layer 5 does not project and then treat it as a fact about the workroom. Adoption is the worked case — the fold projects proposals, ratifications, artifacts, paths and citation edges, and no relation between a proposal and a decision record, so a browser that joined those into \"the decision at this path is adopted\" would be inventing an application fact.\n\nTHREE THINGS I DID NOT CARRY VERBATIM, reported rather than applied silently. The lane's page contained the sentence \"This paragraph previously stated the live-vocabulary rule, and the browser implemented it\". That sentence is false of main's page: it refers to head 3fd75543, and I confirmed with git merge-base --is-ancestor that 3fd75543 is an ancestor of neither main nor the merge-base — it is an abandoned draft. Carrying it would have put a false claim about the page's own history into the reference, so I rewrote the opening as \"The hazard is not only a coding error\" and kept every normative claim, including \"Layer 7 derives no authority of its own\". The other two are spellings, serialisation to serialization and \"for ever\" to forever, because main's page is consistently American.\n\nWHY THIS RESTS ON THE BEHAVIOUR ARTIFACTS. A documentation artifact resting only on the promise can never be flared by a change to the thing it describes. This one rests on internal/workroom 90f43d2b, ui bbdf6c6b and internal/app 222d19f6, so when that behaviour is succeeded this page is told.\n\nGATES RUN BY ME AT THIS EXACT HEAD. go test ./... -count=1 exit 0, 32 ok plus 1 no-test-files, 33 packages, zero failures and zero cached, which matters here because the docset gate caches past documentation edits and a cached pass would predate this change; gofmt -l . empty; go build and go vet exit 0 with no output; npm test 97/97; make ui exit 0 leaving git status empty; make ui-check exit 0; git diff --check exit 0.","body":{"commit":"f6489c78a4e9c11460ebe95e3312f1acd1d0de2c","path":"docs/reference/architecture.md"}}